Subject: [PATCH] the lane sweep: an in-house pre-review across all five
 council lanes

Run because no reviewer has engaged and waiting is not a strategy. Five
lanes, independent and parallel, each against the statute read in full plus
that lane's primary text, the companion, the open queue and the 2026 incident
record. Labelled throughout as issue-spotting, NOT review -- the standing
rule that nobody may claim this text survived review until named reviewers
sign is unchanged, and the sweep says so in its own first paragraph.

SEVEN FINDINGS GRADED FATAL. Four in the tagged statute:

- SEC. 6(a) has no predicate-violation element and no nexus element. Walked
  as a prosecutor must plead it, nothing requires that a SEC. 5 violation
  ever occurred, nothing connects the due-care failure to anything, and 'the
  relevant risk' has no antecedent inside SEC. 6. The one finding where the
  STATE loses rather than the defendant.
- SEC. 5(e) criminalises refusal of a demand the Act never authorises anyone
  to make. The offense is drafted from 21 USC 331(e), which is parasitic on
  section 374's inspection authority; the Act took the offense and left the
  authority behind.
- SEC. 5(b) -- the only offense whose elements match the 2026 conduct -- is
  rule-gated into year four on the Act's own brackets, and the harm tier
  cannot be charged at all because none of the six documented incidents
  produced bodily injury.
- The regulations' control objectives are process without substance: the
  incident record names five contributing factors, none a model property, and
  the intersection with Part 6's six objectives is EMPTY.

And the most consequential finding of all, now OPEN QUESTION 4: SEC. 2(a)'s
'and not otherwise' and SEC. 1(c) tether every duty to in-state deployment,
while the 2026 incidents were EVALUATION conduct, mostly offshore. On the
operative text, five of six documented incidents fall outside the Act at the
threshold. A statute written after those incidents does not reach them.

Three fatal findings are in drafting this queue proposed THIS WEEK:

- CURE 6's deployer carve-out omits 'modifies/fine-tunes/trains upon' while
  Operation 1 reaches 'trained or materially modified'. As drafted, a research
  group publishing a frontier safety protocol self-designates its fine-tunes
  into scope at any compute level, retraction foreclosed -- so the rational
  response is to stop publishing safety frameworks, inverting the Act's
  purpose.
- CURE 7 puts market capitalisation in a criminal scope term, states
  'mass-market scale' with no number and no rule-hook, reaches a lender via
  'finances', self-satisfies its own scale condition so function-plus-scale
  never operates for suppliers, and sweeps in public and academic
  supercomputing. The dollar thresholds themselves survive.
- SEC. 1(b)(1)(B)'s 'does not, standing alone, extend a lineage' is undefined
  in a criminal scope term, and the hostile reading covers every fine-tune of
  every open-weight frontier model.

Queue conformed: a HOLD notice at the head; amendment blocks on CURE 6 and
CURE 7 stating what must change before either lands; CURE 1's held-open
bifurcation ANSWERED NO (breadth on the reporting side is breadth in a crime,
because SEC. 5(c) makes failure to report a custodial offense); OPEN QUESTION
4 opened with drafted amendment text; and a process rule adopted -- every
queue entry must carry an administrative-load note, since CURE 7 added two
rulemakings without the fiscal lane being asked.

Also recorded: what survived five adversarial passes, because that is as
useful as what failed -- SEC. 10(c)(2)(D)'s causation paragraph is better
than its federal donor; SEC. 10(c)(3)'s severalty sentence; SEC. 4(a)'s
exclusion list; SEC. 3(b)'s no-prior-approval rule; SEC. 13(b)(5)'s
element-preservation rule; and the 10^26 threshold, which is enacted law in
two states.

README indexes it at 31a and the council section points reviewers at it
first, with the instruction that where a reviewer disagrees with a sweep
finding, the reviewer is right and the disagreement is a finding.

Tagged v3.4 text unchanged (E10).
